Protocol knowledge from GPL projects
------------------------------------

* OpenRGB - https://gitlab.com/CalcProgrammer1/OpenRGB
  GNU GPL version 2. HyperX Alloy Origins Core controller by Volodymyr
  Nazarchuk (Vavooon), Mike White (kamaaina) and carlos jordao.
  Protocol facts re-implemented: direct mode 0xA2, the GRB packing of the
  380-byte color buffer, brightness 0xA7, command 0x10 and its layout-variant
  byte, and the key-to-LED map (ANSI and ABNT2).

* hxoctkl - https://github.com/identityapproved/hyperx-origins-core-tkl-rgb
  Copyright (C) 2026 identityapproved. GNU GPL version 2 or later.
  Protocol facts and sequences re-implemented: writing animations to the
  onboard profiles (0xA4 / 0xA5 / 0xA0 / 0x93 / 0x94 / 0xB0), the key-press
  layer (0x22 / 0xA3), profile selection (0x15 / 0x95), notifications
  0x70 / 0x71 / 0x73, and the per-LED table of HID usages. The udev rule with
  TAG+="uaccess" also follows this project and OpenRGB.

Because OpenAlloy incorporates knowledge and sequences from GPL projects, it is
distributed under the GPL, version 2 or later, which is compatible with the
MIT-licensed code reused from Linuity, PlasmaNGenuity and hyperx-studio.


Public specifications
---------------------

* HID Usage Tables (USB Implementers Forum), page 0x59 "Lighting and
  Illumination" (HID LampArray), the public standard behind Windows 11 Dynamic
  Lighting. The hid_lamparray driver was written from the specification and
  from the real descriptor of the HP 03f0:098f keyboard (firmware 21.16).

* Linux kernel documentation on hidraw, USB persist and USB/IP, and the
  systemd documentation on systemd-sleep(8), used for the device access, the
  suspend handling and the test lab.


